Pattern : self-contained protocol-seam surface (sibling parity: LegacyAlertStudioRedirect
          0186, LegacyNotificationsRedirect 0187) — no `import Shared`, so the resolver +
          model are host-testable without the Kotlin/Native core. The web source is a
          query-preserving navigation redirect (`useLocation` + `<Navigate
          to={`/notifications/rules${search}`} replace />`); the native parity binds the
          inbound location through a P1/S8 seam, resolves the target via a pure resolver,
          and fires an injected `onRedirect` navigation seam exactly once (the automatic
          replace), rendering the redirect chrome around it so every state is visible while
          the host performs the route change.

NOTE ON NAMESPACING (why this surface ships its own value types):
  Every feature-view + dashboard-widget source compiles into ONE module (TeslaSync). The
  sibling LegacyAlertStudioRedirect.Models.swift already OWNS the un-prefixed `Redirect*`
  type names (RedirectLocation / RedirectDestination / RedirectPhase / …). To avoid
  duplicate-symbol / "ambiguous for type lookup" collisions, EVERY type in this surface is
  namespaced — `AlertRulesRedirect*` for the value types, `LegacyAlertRulesRedirect*` for
  the seams/model/strings. (LegacyNotificationsRedirect 0187 does the same with its own
  names.) This is the exact discipline whose ABSENCE causes the pre-existing baseline
  failure documented in step [8] below.

